emocracy only second so in  other words if I am on the left in a  Venezuela and the extreme left it  doesn't really matter whether my  candidates proposing a democratic or  undemocratic platform they simply voting  for the candidate  and this is something that given the  intuition that isomerize before that  incumbents can take advantage of in  polarized societies a polarized society  is simply one where there's a lot of  people at the extremes and very few in  the middle and so incumbents can count  on the fact that even if they behave  undemocratically their supporters will  not abandon them out of their distaste  for for their undemocratic be right so  let's talk about the middle class and  how does that fit into your model then  so there are or does it not the middle  class all right so so the middle class  is a very tricky category in political  science because there are there's a lot  of things that that could mean and there  are famous statements in political  science like no bourgeoisie no democracy  that effectively claimed that the middle  class in some form is essential for the  survival of democracy except it's as I  said somewhat tricky because it could  mean many many things it could mean  middle income voters or it could mean a  particular class so urban voters for  instance as opposed to rural voters or  the aristocracy and in this context what  is clear that the kind of middle class  that is really important here is  ideological moderates so voters who  don't feel so strongly about either side  either party or either candidate that  they would put that above whether those  candidates are competing democratically  and so how does that play out in  Venezuela  so in Venezuela in this specific  experiment the way it plays out is  really that the more extreme you are as  a voter the more willing you in this  artificial experiment just so just on  paper okay you are willing to say yes  I'm still voting for my partisan  candidate even though I understand that  that person is less democratic than the  opposition's candidate the purpose of  this experiment is to simulate a  real-world scenario where in a real  election in Venezuela just like in this  experiment usually there are candidates  from two bloc's the incumbents blog who  are on the left and the opposition who  are on  right and so the idea is that this  experiment should show effectively that  this is the same trade off that  Venezuelan voters are making in real  life so those who are on the Left if  they are very far on the left are  ultimately saying yes I prefer democracy  to less democracy so I prefer more  democracy to less democracy but given  the difference between these two  candidates and my strong preference for  the more for the leftist candidate well  I'm going to sacrifice democracy and I  guess go for the candidate that's closer  to me ideologically or in the case of  Venezuela it's simply for the one who  redistributes more okay I'm thinking as  you're using the word democracy it could  potentially mean different things to  different people so you're developing a  model starting in Venezuela but  ultimately the model should work in  virtually any country is that correct  so cultures are so different all over  the world obviously so how do you factor  in how someone's what the word democracy  means to someone so even political  scientists disagree about the exact  criteria for what counts as a democratic  country or not and in fact the kind of  undemocratic behavior that I am talking  about is in itself a challenge for our  measurement of democracy so when an  incumbent subverts democracy rather than  it's a military that stages are cool  just like in they did in Turkey this  summer it's not a sharp sudden event it  usually happens over several electoral  cycles so the media the opposition  outside observers have a chance to point  out that undemocratic behavior under  meted undemocratic trends are happening  within a country but it's often very  gradual so it means that a journalist  may get arrested here on there or a  judge that rules in an inconvenient  fashion will get harassed by the  incumbent and in some of the more  extreme cases candidates will simply be  banned from  running or journalists and large numbers  of them can't really arrest it but  precisely because this is gradual it is  often unclear to us at which point a  country becomes so a liberal or  undemocratic that as political  scientists we are warranted in calling  these countries undemocratic so for  instance for a long time in Venezuela  people have disagreed whether ago Chavez  and what uber Chavez was doing in  Venezuela was just a new kind of  democracy maybe populist maybe a  populist democracy and at which point it  really became an undemocratic country  and this is compounded by the fact that  ever since the end of the Cold War we  have very few politicians incumbents who  when they are making these changes and  taking these undemocratic steps who  would explicitly say and that is what  I'm doing I'm on purpose making this  country less democratic much more often  what they are doing is they would say  they are working on an improvement on  democracy so Vladimir Putin in Russia  for instance talks about Russia's  sovereign democracy that is free from  the meddling by foreigners and  Westerners and in Venezuela ago Chavez  was calling his democracy Bolivarian  democracy so this was a concept that he  himself developed and the idea was that  in Venezuela under his government  democracy should combine both democratic  governments so the rule of the majority  but also redistributed elements so the  idea that democracy should be socially  just and what that meant is  redistributed wealth from the rich in  Venezuela to the poor now even in  Venezuela therefore one objection to the  study that I'm making could be that well  maybe when you are in this little survey  experiment saying that one one candidate  is less democratic than the other and I  never say that explicitly but I am  saying for instance this candidate  proposes that the electoral commission  be impartial so presumably something  that's democratic and then another  candidate will say well and I'm  proposing that we just leave the  electoral commission as is so it could  be that maybe though  voters who are voting for the second  type of candidate simply have a  different understanding of democracy and  so what I did as a part of this  experiment is actually ask voters first  so as the respondents in this survey  first well uber Chavez has championed  this idea of Bolivarian democracy what  do you associate it with and so they  were given several choices among them  the elimination of the inequalities  between the rich and poor but also more  standard understandings of democracy  such as freedom of electoral competition  civil liberties checks and balances and  so on and so these voters correctly I  think about 70% of them said yes when we  talking about Bolivar and democracy it's  definitely about reducing inequalities  between the rich and the poor but the  next question was and so which aspect of  democracy is most important for you and  their most voters say either the  competitiveness of Elections or civil  liberties or checks and balances or  political accountability only I think  about 9% say that it is the reduction of  the inequities between the rich and the  poor so it seems like in spite of all  kinds of possible confusions even  ordinary Venezuelans know that democracy  primarily means electoral competition  and that that competition should be free  and fair okay so as you're developing  this model ultimately what do you hope  to achieve so I hope to propose one way  in which we can explain why it is that  somebody like Putin in Russia or Chavez  in Venezuela aired Iran and Turkey are  at first able to get democratically  elected and then gradually and in plain  sight so as I mentioned this is not  something that happens secretly people  write about this in newspapers  journalists write about it the  opposition complains about it both at  home and abroad  and then for an observers comment on  this and nonetheless these incumbents  stay in power they gradually subvert  democracy and not only that they do so  while maintaining popularity so one  thing that's very unique  these cases is that all three of these  cases that I listed poutine Chavez air  Doan are extremely popular and they were  very popular when they got elected at  first and they remained popular so one  of the puzzles is why is it that voters  who presumably value democracy would  nonetheless vote and support candidates  who clearly are undemocratic in how they  have acted in office and the answer is  in this explanation that I'm proposing  is that in a polarized society even an  incumbent that is behaving  undemocratically you will still maintain  a significant support precisely because  it's a polarized society and so those  who support him are not going to abandon  him for a challenger because in a  polarized society there are these huge  differences between between voters and  voters are unlikely to defect to a  challenger from an incumbent that they  support very interesting and I know this  paper you're working on is part of a  larger project a larger book project  you're working on do you wanna tell us a  little bit about that so I'm currently  working on a book democratization in the  age of elections and the key idea for  the book is to explain how either  transitions to democracy or what I just  spoke about that is the breakdown of  democracy happens after the end of the  Cold War and the key distinctions there  is that whereas before the end of the  Cold War we could easily tear apart a  dictatorship from democracy by simply  asking a simple question well does that  country have elections so we had  countries like the Soviet Union that  would if have any elections this would  be such that incumbents would be the  Communist Party would be winning with 99  percent of the vote so it will be  meaningless elections and then we would  have democratic countries that would  have meaningful elections but something  that happened after the end of the Cold  War and that is connected to the trends  that I was summarizing before is that  today almost every country has some kind  of elections the few that don't have  them like Saudi Arabia or maybe China  seem like they are actually some  anachronistic cases  have been inherited from the Cold War  air but nonetheless we do have  distinctions between dictatorships and  democracies we know that electoral  competition in most of Europe and in the  United States warrants that we call  these countries democracies whereas the  kind of electoral competition that is  currently happening in Russia Venezuela  or Turkey has been so much skewed in  favor of the incumbent that we can no  longer call these countries democratic  so people have all kinds of labels for  them  people call them hybrid regimes  electoral authoritarian regimes or  competitive authoritarian regimes but  one thing that unifies them is that they  do have elections but those elections  are not competitive enough so that we  can call these countries democracies and  so what this means is that if we want to  understand transitions between democracy  and dictatorship today it is not going  to happen where we'll have a  dictatorship that doesn't have elections  and that dictatorship is at some moment  going to transition into country a  democracy that will have elections but  rather that a country that has highly  imperfect uncompetitive elections is  going to make those elections fair and  competitive and what as political  scientists we don't understand well it's  precisely what governs that transition  why is it that countries some countries  are like Russia whereas others are  perfectly competitive extremely  important work you're doing thank you  for being here with us today thank you  for more information about professor  slow leak and his research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  [Music]  you
 

